# Resolution
Fixed in commit [41bd85fd](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/41bd85fd) (`Bound placed shard repair hint queue`), after the durable repair queue work that made PgStore repair rows the authoritative record.

This finding was partially stale after durable placed-shard repair rows were added: foreground reads no longer rely on the in-memory queue as the only repair evidence, because `schedule_placed_segment_shard_repair` records a durable row before adding the local wake hint. The remaining valid issue was that the wake hint queue itself was still an unbounded `VecDeque`/`HashSet`.

The fix caps the local in-memory placed-shard repair hint queue at `LOCAL_PLACED_SEGMENT_SHARD_REPAIR_HINT_QUEUE_LIMIT` entries. Duplicate hints are still deduplicated, and once the hint queue is full, new non-duplicate hints are dropped after the durable repair row has already been recorded. That bounds process-memory growth while preserving repair evidence in the durable queue for workers/scrubbers to list and claim.

Regression coverage was added in `placed_segment_shard_repair_hint_queue_is_bounded`, and the existing read-recovery scheduling regression still confirms normal recoverable reads enqueue one durable repair row and one local hint.

# Summary
Introduced an availability bug: read-discovered repair work is scheduled into an unbounded in-memory queue without capacity controls. Deduplication only limits duplicate entries for the exact same shard repair item, not attacker-driven growth across many objects or segments.
Normal object reads now call the placed-segment read path with repair scheduling enabled. When recovery succeeds after observing missing, wrong-sized, or corrupt shards, each affected shard is enqueued into a new runtime repair queue. That queue is backed by a VecDeque plus HashSet and only deduplicates identical work items; it does not enforce any global or per-PG limit, does not drop or throttle work, and does not apply backpressure to foreground reads. An authenticated reader, or an anonymous reader of a public bucket, could repeatedly read many distinct objects/segments during a node/disk failure or corruption event and force the server to retain one repair item per affected shard/segment. Because the queue is in process memory, this can lead to memory exhaustion and service unavailability.

