## Report
Validated. Rubric-guided checks found that normal reads call `try_read_placed_segment_stored_bytes_into(..., true)` at `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:6813`; successful recovery then iterates `repair_targets` and enqueues each at `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:7261-7264`; the queue state is only `VecDeque<PlacedSegmentShardRepairWorkItem>` plus `HashSet<...>` at `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:962-965`; enqueue only exact-deduplicates with `HashSet::insert` and then unconditionally `push_back`s at `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:1139-1150`; object payload reads reach this storage read path at `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:1385-1401`. I added a bounded PoC unit test that creates 64 distinct committed segments, corrupts shard 0 for each, reads each segment successfully through the foreground read path, and drains the repair queue. Command output: `POC queued_repair_items=64 distinct_segments=64`, proving one retained in-memory item per distinct segment/shard. Direct crash/OOM was not forced to avoid destabilizing the container; the bounded PoC did not crash but demonstrates linear unbounded growth. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). LLDB was available and confirmed the exact foreground read-to-queue chain: breakpoint at `local.rs:1148` (`state.work_queue.push_back(work_item)`) showed stack frames `LocalClusterRuntimeState::enqueue_placed_segment_shard_repair` -> `StorageCluster::enqueue_placed_segment_shard_repair` at `cluster.rs:7335` -> `try_read_placed_segment_recovery_into` at `cluster.rs:7263` -> `try_read_placed_segment_stored_bytes_into(..., schedule_repair_on_recovery=true)` -> `read_segment_payload_stored_bytes_into` at `cluster.rs:6813` -> PoC test. This supports the suspected availability bug: deduplication only suppresses exact duplicate work items, while attacker-driven reads of many distinct degraded/corrupt segments accumulate queue entries without capacity/backpressure.

