# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Kept as medium. Static evidence and the provided executable validation PoC support a real in-scope availability vulnerability: normal GetObject reads can reach an unbounded in-memory repair queue and distinct degraded segments create distinct retained work items. However, this is not high/critical because the impact is denial of service only, not data compromise or privilege escalation; the attacker must already be able to read the objects or rely on public-read ACLs; and the bug only triggers during recoverable degraded/corrupt shard conditions with enough distinct segments. HTTP concurrency limits reduce burst load but do not bound total queued repair work, so they mitigate likelihood somewhat but do not eliminate the issue.
## Likelihood
high - The read path is network reachable in normal deployments and may be anonymous for public-read buckets, but exploitation depends on a recoverable shard/node/disk corruption or failure state, many distinct readable segments, and queue growth outpacing any repair/drain behavior. The repository default listen address is localhost and no deployment ingress was found. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - A successful attack can exhaust argmin-s3 process memory and make the S3 service unavailable. The impact is availability only, with no demonstrated confidentiality, integrity, code execution, or authorization bypass impact, and likely affects the single service/process rather than a broader fleet by itself.
## Assumptions
- argmin-s3 is deployed with its HTTP S3 endpoint reachable by the attacker, even though the repository default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000
- the attacker has normal GetObject access to many distinct objects or segments, or the target bucket/object ACLs allow anonymous public reads
- at least one storage shard/node/disk is in a recoverable degraded, missing, wrong-sized, or corrupt state while reads are served
- repair queue consumption, if externally driven in a deployment, is absent or slower than attacker-driven enqueue growth
- HTTP GetObject reachability
- valid read authorization or anonymous public-read object access
- many distinct readable objects or segments
- recoverable missing, wrong-sized, corrupt, or unreadable shard condition
## Path
Attacker GET /bucket/key(s)
  -> server-http GetObject dispatch
  -> Coordinator::get_object / ReadHandle segments
  -> read_segment_payload_stored_bytes_into(..., schedule=true)
  -> recovery observes missing/corrupt shard(s)
  -> enqueue_placed_segment_shard_repair
  -> VecDeque + HashSet grows without capacity/backpressure
  -> memory exhaustion / service unavailable
