## Narrative
The finding is real and reachable. Normal segment reads call try_read_placed_segment_stored_bytes_into with repair scheduling enabled. If direct reads fail and recovery succeeds, missing/wrong-sized/corrupt/unreadable shards are collected as repair_targets and each target is enqueued. The queue is only an in-memory VecDeque plus HashSet; enqueue exact-deduplicates identical work items, then unconditionally push_back's the new item with no capacity or backpressure. HTTP GetObject reaches this storage read path after authentication/authorization, and repository tests show anonymous public-read objects are valid. The provided validation PoC queued 64 repair items for 64 distinct degraded segments, demonstrating linear growth. Severity remains medium: impact is service availability loss, but exploitation needs read access or public-read objects plus a recoverable degraded/corrupt storage condition and many distinct segments.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication for private object access
- Bucket/object ACL and policy authorization before reads
- Anonymous access only for public-read objects
- Connection semaphore and request semaphore bound concurrent HTTP load
- Default ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR is 127.0.0.1:9000
- Exact-item HashSet deduplication prevents duplicate identical repair items but not distinct segment growth
## Blindspots
- Static-only review did not observe a real deployment, so actual ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR exposure, TLS termination, reverse proxy controls, and object counts are unknown.
- No Kubernetes, Docker, Terraform, or ingress manifests were found in the repository checkout, so public exposure cannot be confirmed from repo artifacts.
- The exact behavior of any out-of-band repair consumer in production deployments was not fully established; repository evidence shows the queue itself is unbounded and only directly drained by try_take_placed_segment_shard_repair_work.
- The validation PoC demonstrates linear queue growth but intentionally did not force OOM or crash the container.
